-- title:
--   Invariance of the zero-scheme ideal under isomorphisms
-- statement:
--   Let $X$ be a scheme (in a fixed universe), let $M$ and $M'$ be objects of the monoidal category `X.Modules` of modules on $X$, let $s : \mathbb{1} \to M$ be a morphism from the monoidal unit (the structure sheaf) to $M$, and let $f : M \to M'$ be a morphism which is an isomorphism. For such a section $t : \mathbb{1} \to N$, the ideal sheaf datum `zeroSchemeIdeal t` is defined as the infimum, inside `X.IdealSheafData`, of the set of those ideal sheaf data $J$ satisfying: for every affine open $U$ of $X$, the ideal `coeffIdeal t U` — the ideal of $\Gamma(X, U)$ generated by the range of the coefficient map `coeff t U` — is contained in the ideal $J.\mathrm{ideal}\,U$. The assertion is that the two ideal sheaf data obtained in this way from $s$ followed by $f$ and from $s$ itself coincide: $\mathrm{zeroSchemeIdeal}(s \gg f) = \mathrm{zeroSchemeIdeal}(s)$. Thus the ideal attached to a section is unchanged by transporting the section along an isomorphism of the target module.
--
--   Classically this says that the zero scheme (divisor of zeros, when the module is a line bundle) of a section depends only on the isomorphism class of the pair consisting of the module and the section; in particular it is insensitive to multiplying the section by a global unit or to an identification of the module with a twist of the structure sheaf. It is the invariance invoked in statements about relative effective Cartier divisors and divisors of zeros, and about supports of sections of line bundles on relative Picard and abelian-scheme charts.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AlgebraicGeometry_Scheme_Modules_zeroSchemeIdeal_comp_eq_of_isIso.lean

